# goal src/metrics.js

What this bridge spent, in the one format that scrapes. Off unless
`server.metricsAddr` names an address.

# decision The bridge is the measuring point, not the agents {#why_here}

Every coding CLI has its own telemetry and no two are alike. codex exports
`codex.turn.token_usage` over OTLP if you write an `[otel]` block into its
`config.toml`; Claude Code exports `claude_code.token.usage` if you set
`CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TELEMETRY` and three `OTEL_*` variables. Different mechanism,
different metric namespace, different labels, different lifecycle — per agent,
repeated for every agent added later. The registry lists thirty-eight.

This bridge is the one component all of them pass through, and it ALREADY
normalizes their counters: `Usage` off the `session/prompt` response, turned from
session-cumulative into per-turn by `deltaUsage`. So exporting from here is one
mechanism and one label set for every agent alike, and an agent added tomorrow is
measured on the day it is added rather than when someone gets round to wiring its
vendor's exporter.

**No dependency.** The Prometheus text format is a line per sample. A client
library would be a fifth package to concatenate strings — see [#deps](../_.hint).

# rule Where each number is recorded, and why not anywhere else {#record_sites}

Two call sites, and the split is not arbitrary.

| what | where | why there |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `turns_total`, `turn_duration_seconds` | `Agent#turn` | the one funnel every caller path goes through, and the ONLY place that sees a turn that threw |
| tokens, cost, context fill | `settleUsage` in server.js | runs exactly once per turn and holds the only per-turn figures that exist |

`settleUsage` reaches the recorder as `sessions.metrics?.` rather than taking it as
an argument, because there are five call sites for it and threading a parameter
through all five is five chances to miss one. The store already owns the
per-conversation baselines and the agent name, which is exactly what a per-turn
sample is made of.

Recording BEFORE the settling would export the whole conversation's spend on every
turn — that is [#usage_is_cumulative](openai.js.hint), one layer down.

`outcome` is named after the STATUS CONTRACT this bridge already publishes to a
failover chain — `rate_limited`, `unauthenticated`, `cancelled`, `unavailable`,
`timeout`, `error` — so the metric and the router agree about what happened instead
of inventing a second taxonomy. See [#limit_detection](../_.hint).

# invariant Silence must not read as zero {#unmeasured_not_free}

`turns_total` is incremented for every turn. `tokens_total` and
`usage_reported_total` only when the agent reported counts, and no zero-valued
series is emitted in their place.

That gap IS the signal. An agent whose turns climb while its tokens stay flat is
not a cheap agent, it is an unmeasured one, and a dashboard that showed it as `0`
would be confidently wrong. Do not "fix" the missing series by initialising
counters to zero.

# bad Two labels that must never be added {#label_hazards}

- **A conversation id.** Unbounded cardinality — the entire point of a conversation
  here is that there can be arbitrarily many, and each one is short-lived.
- **A second identity column.** In this bridge the OpenAI `model` IS the agent name,
  so a `model` label would repeat `agent` exactly. Which CLI sits behind the name is
  worth knowing and is what the operator's own labels are for. `agent` and `model`
  are rejected as operator label names at config time for the same reason.

# rule Operator labels are declared, not discovered {#operator_labels}

`labels:` on an agent is a free-form map merged into every sample that agent
produces. It exists for `account`.

Which subscription an agent spends is a property of the login in the home directory
it was spawned with. No ACP message reports it; no probe would make it
authoritative; and two agents may legitimately share one account while two others
do not. Only the operator knows, so the operator declares it — and having accepted
a declared value, `plan`, `owner`, `host` and whatever comes next cost nothing
further.

Values are escaped for the exposition format (`\\`, `"`, newline) and label NAMES
are validated at config time, because a broken label breaks the whole scrape rather
than one line of it.

# rule Quota is not here, and that is deliberate {#no_quota}

How much of a subscription is LEFT is not in ACP for any agent — exhaustion arrives
as an unstructured error string, which is why `server.limitPatterns` exists at all.
The uniform signal is therefore `acp2api_turns_total{outcome="rate_limited"}`: it
says a quota ran out, not how much remains.

Anything better is one vendor's private file format — codex writes
`rate_limits.primary.used_percent` into its rollout JSONL, Claude Code has its own
— and a protocol bridge that grows a reader per vendor stops being a protocol
bridge. That belongs in whatever deployment cares, beside the agent.

# rule Counters are process-lifetime and in memory {#no_persistence}

A restart resets them, which is what `rate()` and `increase()` are built to
survive. Persisting would add a store and buy nothing a scrape interval cannot
already see.

The registry is built whether or not `metricsAddr` is set: recording costs a map
write, and an operator who turns the listener on wants counters that have been
running, not counters that start at the moment they looked.

# done Acceptance

- tokens are exported per kind, per agent, settled rather than cumulative
- an agent that reports no usage emits no token series at all
- operator labels appear on every sample and never leak between agents
- a rate-limited turn is counted once, as `rate_limited`, and not also as `ok`
- quotes and newlines in a label value cannot break the exposition format
- `/metrics` answers `text/plain; version=0.0.4`; every other path is 404
